{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/585211-hunter-contract","id":585211,"title":"Hunter Contract","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-09T14:38:11-07:00","id":585211,"title":"Hunter Contract","content":"So I was backing up files today and stumbled across this. I wrote up a series of cross-over ideas for Hunter, and one was for Changeling.<br /> Basically, the idea was that some Fae have deals that they allow mortals to swear when they are hunting monsters. Seems reasonable that the Fae are quite willing to even the odds for a good story.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>The Oathsworn</b><br /> One way to become ennobled is to tap an ancient covenant that promises power in exchange for some specified service.<br /> <br /> <i> &quot;I come to you under the ancient compact, to become the Penitent Hero.&quot;<br /> &quot;You cannot. You have been wronged, surely, but you have nothing to repent, yet.&quot;<br /> &quot;I'll swear that other oath, then.&quot;<br /> &quot;Young hero, you need to understand what you swear.&quot;<br /> &quot;Don't you want people to swear these things? I thought the compact was considered a trap for the unwary or desperate.&quot;<br /> &quot;I warn you a second time: Know the oath. It is recorded in its entirety just there.&quot;<br /> <br /> The man reads it, briefly. He then rereads it more thoroughly.<br /> <br /> &quot;mm. the hero is a... powers... must complete the sworn task or forfeit the pledge... mm. That's not nearly so onerous as I expected, actually.&quot;<br /> &quot;Hero, read it again and think on it before deciding. Do not swear or pledge in haste.&quot;<br /> &quot;Sir, I need power to save my daughter. I have no choice. On my honor and her life I need the power to save her.&quot;<br /> &quot;Yes. You certainly need power, but is this the path you truly should tread?&quot;<br /> &quot;Are you rejecting my oath or trying to talk me out of it?&quot;<br /> &quot;Neither. I want you to walk away and seek a better path if you cannot bear the costs of this one. Stop and truly consider your options, here.&quot;<br /> &quot;I have. I will swear.&quot;<br /> &quot;What is your pledge? What is your sworn action?&quot;<br /> &quot;It's unclear in the oath what it can be.&quot;<br /> &quot;The joy of your daughter is a fine pledge for you, hero. The pain of loss runs so deeply in your bones, as it is.&quot;<br /> &quot;You will take away all joy from my daughter in exchange for letting me save her? That is a horrible choice to make!&quot;<br /> &quot;You have not understood, I see. A pledge is given back when a contract is fulfilled.&quot;<br /> &quot;I see. So you would allow me to swear to save her with a pledge of the joy of her?&quot;<br /> &quot;This I would accept, but be certain you understand. You will never be able to take joy in her until you succeed. If you should fail, it cannot return.&quot;<br /> &quot;What they are doing to her robs me of this joy already. Let me swear!&quot;<br /> <br /> The noble smiled and watched his new Hero of the Thorns pledge himself and then leave, intent of rescuing his daughter. His servant appeared and asked &quot;May I inquire, fair one?&quot;<br /> &quot;You may.&quot;<br /> &quot;You counseled patience thrice, yet you captured many with sly words in the past. Clever speech was always your ally, yet you let this one argue a deal barely in your favor. Pray explain?&quot;<br /> <br /> The noble smiled indulgently.<br /> &quot;Without his joy, he can recall the joy, he just cannot feel it. His wellspring is dry. His daughter's love is there; His love is there; but neither bring him joy. In his low hour of need, he must rely on the power I gave him. That will be reward enough for me, and yet that is not all.&quot;<br /> <br /> He graced his servant with impossible ugly-yet-beautiful smile and continued:<br /> &quot;If he fails, he knows I dealt fairly with him and warned him of his fate. The taste of that will be exquisite until he dies.<br /> If he wins the day, I return his joy, which he exults in, and will consider himself the victor in our exchange, except for one thing: I will always share in his triumph. He needed my help--the one who dealt fairly.<br /> Either way, he lives a good story and one that makes the mortals think they can win; either way, I win twice.<br /> I am not a mortal, my good servant: it is enough that I win; I need not ensure that he loses.&quot;<br /> &quot;Thank you, kind master, for explaining you brilliance.&quot;<br /> &quot;Come taste this joy, beloved servant. It is a perfect blend of joy and sorrow. How he could part with it is one more mystery of the ages.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> <br /> This seemed to require something a bit beyond the basic oath rules, so I figured I'd post it here and see what people thought.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Grump, grouse, and/or gripe.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/672-thenate","name":"thenate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar672_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}